WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.010
You're clearly such a strong person,

2
00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:06.190 line:15%
but why did you decide to join the military

3
00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:07.560 line:15%
in the first place?

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.910 line:15%
Oh, that's a funny story.

5
00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:15.150 line:15%
I decided to join the military because I have a cousin.

6
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:16.840 line:15%
She was in the Air Force.

7
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.500 line:15%
Now you heard me, I said she.

8
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.910 line:15%
She was in the Air Force.

9
00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.140 line:15%
She was my role model.

10
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.350
I saw her in the uniform.

11
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.070
I saw her going places all over,

12
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:28.380
and it looked so glamorous,

13
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.720
and it looked like, in my eyes,

14
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.770
that the military was the place to be.

15
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:36.500
And she looked, not just, she looked so studious.

16
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.410
She looked important,

17
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.110
and I wanted to be like that.

18
00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.372
So that's one of the reasons why I joined.

19
00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:43.281
(audience applauding)

20
00:00:43.281 --> 00:00:47.090
Once you got in it, and you saw how it really functioned,

21
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.710
and you got onto the inside,

22
00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.130
and you weren't just looking at the outside,

23
00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.860
how did you find that women were treated?

24
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.010
So I love the military.

25
00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.720
I wanna say that first.

26
00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.610
I love the United States military,

27
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.753
but (Donna laughs) actually being in,

28
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.370
we as women, we go to the frontline.

29
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.890
We are in war,

30
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.830
and we're not recognized.

31
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.620
There was times in the beginning when I first joined

32
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:15.950
I didn't even get a chance to do the job

33
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.790
that I was trained for.

34
00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.630
We don't even get recognized outside the military.

35
00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.610
We do every single job.

36
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.199
Every single job that the military offers, we do.

37
00:01:26.199 --> 00:01:29.410
(audience applauding)

38
00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.020
Is it true that you had an interesting interaction

39
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.380
with a veteran after you returned home?

40
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.840
So not just on the frontlines but back at home.

41
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.723
Oh yes.

42
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.380
My wife and I, we were,

43
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.910
we pulled up to the gas station,

44
00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.690
and I have disabled veteran plates.

45
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.613
I have a wounded warrior sticker in my front window.

46
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.340
Obviously a veteran looking vehicle.

47
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.120
And a gentleman was behind me and pumping gas,

48
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.220
and he finished pumping his gas and pulled in front.

49
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.303
And he came to salute me and said,

50
00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.200
make sure you tell your husband

51
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.100
that I said thank him for his service.

52
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.310
In turn, I turned to him and I told him, you know what?

53
00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.450
Thank you for your service,

54
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.094
and I'm the veteran.

55
00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:17.808
So.
(audience applauding)

56
00:02:17.808 --> 00:02:20.160
And what was his reaction in that moment?

57
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.693
I think he was shocked and embarrassed,

58
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.020
and he just drove off.

59
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.550
I first got to know about Wounded Warrior

60
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.240
about 10 years ago.

61
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.607
A friend of mine is a big participator,

62
00:02:33.607 --> 00:02:37.110
and it brought the project to my attention.

63
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:42.110
And when I see the way your life has transformed with them,

64
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:49.360
this is so much more than a topical association.

65
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.520
It's like, it seemed like they did give you a door

66
00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.170
that you walked through,

67
00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.820
and on the other side of that door

68
00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.380
was a life waiting for you.

69
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.570
Let me tell you.

70
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.250
I can't do anything,

71
00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.590 line:15%
but when I think of Wounded Warrior Project,

72
00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:10.590 line:15%
I smile because, just like you said,

73
00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.960 line:15%
Wounded Warrior Project is not just an organization.

74
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:13.793 line:15%
Yes.

75
00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:16.933 line:15%
It's an organization that empowers women.

76
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.530 line:15%
This organization literally gave me my life back.

77
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.040 line:15%
It didn't gimme my life back.

78
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.040 line:15%
It actually boosted and kicked forward my life.

79
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.060
Without Wounded Warrior Project,

80
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.460
I probably wouldn't be here.

81
00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:39.100
Wounded Warrior Project gave me a community, gave me family.

82
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.860
When you're in the military, you gain a family.

83
00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.110
And sometimes being injured and getting out of the military,

84
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.380
you kinda lose those connections, lose that family.

85
00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.050
And every single event I've ever been on

86
00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.370
in Wounded Warrior Project,

87
00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.210
it's like going to a family reunion,

88
00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.730
and I have that family again.

89
00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.160
I have those connections.

90
00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.680
It gives you that comradery.

91
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.770
And without Wounded Warrior Project,

92
00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.610
I know myself and a lot of others wouldn't be here.

93
00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.360
Well, I'm so glad that you are here, Donna.

94
00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.220
And on International Women's Day,

95
00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.530
your fierceness, your strength, your life choices,

96
00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.800
your life attitude, choosing to go and serve and protect,

97
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.260
choosing to be on a high road

98
00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.190
when every other option seems probably easier,

99
00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.420
you are such an inspiration.

100
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.253
Thank you.

101
00:04:30.253 --> 00:04:33.420
And I'm so glad to meet you and hear your story.

102
00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.460
Thank you, Donna.

103
00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.293
Thank you.

104
00:04:35.293 --> 00:04:37.940
And to learn more about how Wounded Warrior Project

105
00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.420 line:15%
is helping lift up female veterans,

106
00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.783 line:15%
visit more WoundedWarriorProject.org/EmpowerWomenVets.

107
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:47.902 line:15%
Thank you, Donna.

108
00:04:47.902 --> 00:04:50.485
(upbeat music)

